c=Table(columns=[{Term:"Term",What it means",Typical effect on claim"},{Term","Deductible","A fixed amount you must pay before the insurer pays any admissible claim. In indemnity plans, it is a rupee. [3]","The insurer starts paying paying only after deductible is crossed."},{Term:"Co-payment","A percentage of the admissible claim that you must bear. It does not reduce the sum insured, but reduces what the insurer pays for that claim.","You share the bill in a fixed ratio, such as 0% or20%."},{Term:"Waiting period","A period after policy start during which certain claims are not payable. Standard policies generally have a30-day initial waiting period, and pre-existing-disease waiting periods are commonly cappedat366 months in newer IRDA-regulated-products [7]","Claims during the waiting period may be fully rejected."},{Term:"Room-rent limit","A cap on the hospital room charge covered by the policy, often linked to a percentage of the sum insured. [12]","If you choose alier room, some policies reduce payment for other other hospital charges in the same proportion."},{Term:"Exclusions","Treatments or situations the policy does not cover at all, such as pre-existing disease during the waiting period, many non-medical items, cosmetic, care, and other exclusions. [13][2]","The insurer pays nothing for excluded items or events."}]}
